= B4 Outline

Do welfare at the market level.~

+ Show one exchange, setting the price very low with a price control, thinking through quantity exchanged, and so on.
+ Then thinking about incentives in the price control, and then a move to equilibrium, and how welfare changed.
+ Then more quickly show a high price, doing it with TS maybe here.
+ Then force a few more people to exchange and see what happens to TS.
+ Then do first welfare theorem.

~

== Sep 23 | Used in Class

+ Review B3 in seven stages, with Exercise B3 Q2 before equilibrium.
+ The gains from trade. CS and PS on one exchange become total surplus.
+ A price floor at \$6. The lost gains, then one pair that can’t trade.
+ A price ceiling at \$3. The lost gains, then one pair that can’t trade. Exercise B4 Q1.
+ Give that pair the trade. The market without controls allows all beneficial trades.
+ Trades beyond equilibrium. Negative TS.
+ First welfare theorem. Exercise B4 Q2.

== Sep 22 | Proposed, Back Half

+ How much does the market benefit buyers and sellers. Total surplus.
+ Can we increase it by raising the price? DWL is the TS we could have had. Zoom into a pair that doesn’t trade because of the price control.
+ Then lower the price. Show the DWL again with a specific pair.
+ Give them the trade. TS is maximized by allowing all these people to trade.
+ We don’t want anything more. Force the next pair to trade and they lose benefit. Negative TS.

